{#
    What the trajectory is a continuation of.

    The same chart as the spending trajectory below it, over the twelve months
    that have already happened rather than the twelve ahead — one payload
    builder, one partial, one set of rules for the axis and the peak, so the
    two pictures on this screen are read the same way and the eye can carry a
    figure from one to the other.

    Reconstructed rather than recorded, and by the same code the year-over-year
    card further down is totalled from: one walk of the household, one set of
    historic prices, so the two cannot disagree about what a given charge cost.

    Their windows are not the same window, and the link in the header is not a
    claim that they add up to each other. This is twelve calendar months, which
    is the only thing a chart with a month axis can be; the comparison below is
    a rolling year against the rolling year before it. They differ at the edges
    by however far into this month we are, which is exactly what the part-month
    note under the figures is about.
#}
{% import 'partials/spend_chart.twig' as trend %}

<section class="card chart-card" id="analytics-history">
    <header class="card-header">
        <h2>{{ t('stats.history') }}</h2>
        <a class="button button-quiet" href="#analytics-year-over-year">{{ t('stats.year_over_year') }}</a>
    </header>

    <p class="muted">{{ t('stats.history_note') }}</p>

    {{ trend.spend_chart(
        history,
        'analytics-history-data',
        t('dashboard.spend_history_alt', {'currency': history.currency}),
        t('dashboard.history_part_month_note'),
        t('stats.history_unconvertible', {
            'currencies': history.unconvertible|join(', '),
            'currency': history.currency,
        })
    ) }}
</section>
